The local inversion changes packet counts as well as radii. A first nonzero term \(y\sim a x^{-p/q}\) turns a \(q\)-disc orbit into \(p\) child discs; orders scale by \(u_s q/p\). The orbit’s total major contribution therefore scales by \(u_s\). This explains how R009 and R050 can retain 8 despite having different packet partitions.

